from @piton/belay import Skill

// Base for every crux skill. It is abstract, so it never
// compiles or is emitted on its own. Concrete skills declare with the
// skill keyword and extend this anchor, giving their own description,
// useWhen and prompt. Shared guidance lives in named properties rather
// than prompt: the skill keyword puts Skill last in the chain, so its
// inherited null prompt default would override a prompt set here,
// while named properties inherit cleanly and are serialized as
// sections after each concrete skill's own prompt.
export abstract anchor PitonSkillBase extends Skill as skill:
    workingWithPiton:
        Start by finding the project. Open piton.config.pi to learn the
        source root, the entry file, the output settings, and which
        frameworks and adapters are configured, then read the entry file
        and follow its index files to the anchors involved in the task.
        Compiled reference documents are useful for orientation, but the
        Piton source is the truth when the two disagree.

        Treat the .pi files as the only source of truth. Never hand-edit
        generated output, including generated guidance files, generated
        skill, command and agent files, renderer output, and anything
        tracked in .piton/manifest.json. Make every lasting change in
        the .pi source and rebuild so the generated files follow.

        Follow references lazily. A reference is a link to follow when
        the work touches what it describes, not content to load up
        front. Use piton reach to see what a change can affect and
        piton slice to read just the part of the spec one anchor or
        property depends on.

        Work in a verified loop. Make the smallest change that covers
        the task, then run piton format and piton check, fix anything
        they report in the source, run piton build, and inspect the
        diff of the generated files before reporting the result. Say
        plainly what the compiler reported rather than claiming success
        it did not confirm.

    groundRules:
        - Read Piton fluency once per context. Do not re-read it for each skill or task in the same context.
        - Read the relevant source and compiled reference before changing anything.
        - Keep changes in the Piton source, scoped to what the task asks for.
        - Prefer the conventions already used by neighbouring files over new ones.
        - Report compiler errors and warnings exactly as they are, with their files.
